# sprint_mem_b6_fs.oot — MEM: lazy ret_table + fs_mkdir_p / fs_copy_tree
# Date: 2026-08-14
# Role: MEM. This file only. Did not edit product. Did not run oodac check oodac/main.oo.
# Method: file read + ripgrep. No new clock. No ci_product.oo.
# Pin used (not re-timed): INT sprint_int_open_prove.oot 26.30s / 12.26 GiB RSS.
# Marker: SPRINT_MEM_B6_FS_2026-08-14
## Verdict
Lazy `ret_table` is **defer-until-first-non-cap-miss**, not delete-forever.
The first non-cap lookup in the lazy walkers still calls `build_fn_ret_table`
and still concatenates the **full** fname:Ret String. That is the honest
B6 leftover for later 12 GiB work.

`fs_mkdir_p` is **not** an unbounded tree walk. `fs_copy_tree` **is** the
same class as `fs_walk`: self-recursion, no depth cap, no visited set.

| Site | Asked | Disk | Heap / stack class | Close? |
|------|-------|------|--------------------|--------|
| lazy `ret_table` | table gone forever? | **no** — deferred | first miss = full concat table | **OPEN** (B6 leftover) |
| first non-cap miss | still full table? | **yes** | same `build_fn_ret_table` as before | **yes, residual** |
| sibling walkers | also lazy? | **no** | still eager at entry / in-loop | **OPEN** |
| `fs_mkdir_p` | unbounded recurse like walk? | **no** | linear prefix of one path | **not same** |
| `fs_copy_tree` | unbounded recurse like walk? | **yes** | same as `fs_walk` + file copy | **OPEN** (same residual) |
| 12 GiB check RSS | new number? | **not measured** | still the B8 pin | **NOT CLOSED** |

Do not say B6 is cheap. Do not say the table is gone. Do not invent a new RSS.

## 1 — lazy ret_table does not remove the table
Live shape (read this turn). Two walkers only:

| Walker | Entry | Sentinel | Build sites |
|--------|-------|----------|-------------|
| `typecheck_let_ann_call_init` | `tc_call_let.oo:20` `let mut ret_table: String = ""` | `ret_table == ""` | `:77-78` annotated let-init, **else** of `cap_builtin_ret` |
| `typecheck_return_and_assign_calls` | `tc_call_ret.oo:14` same `""` | same | `:131-132` `return f(...);` miss; `:185-186` mut `x = f(...)` miss |

Comments match the code: `tc_call_let.oo:12` "built on first non-cap miss,
not at entry"; `tc_call_ret.oo:5` "lazy on first non-cap miss".

What lazy is

- Start empty. Cap names (`cap_builtin_ret` ≠ `""`) never assign `ret_table`.
- First miss: `ret_table = build_fn_ret_table(toks)`.
- Later misses in **that same call** see `ret_table != ""` and reuse it.
- `build_fn_ret_table` (`tc_tables_ret.oo:12-53`) always starts `table = "\t"`
  and returns at least `"\t"`. After one build, `== ""` is false for the rest
  of the walker. Nothing writes `ret_table` back to `""`. The table is **kept**,
  not dropped, not rebuilt per miss.

What lazy is not

- Not "table removed from the compiler."
- Not "cap-only check never pays a table" on the product rail (siblings still do).
- Not a List[String] rewrite. Still whole-table String concat:
  `table = table + fname + ":" + ret + "\t"` per `fn` with ARROW IDENT
  (`tc_tables_ret.oo:45`). O(F²) copies for F named returns.
- `resolve_type_alias` (`tc_types_core.oo:12-49`) then `contains` + char walk
  of that String. First non-cap miss still pays that scan.

Unannotated `let x = f(` (`tc_call_let.oo:109-118`) still only calls
`cap_builtin_ret` and drops `gty`. It does **not** build the table and does
**not** fall back to `call_ret_type_at`. Cap-only unannotated lets stay
cheap **inside this walker**. One annotated `let x: T = user_fn(` in the
same module still builds the **full** table for every later miss.

`cap_builtin_ret` (`tc_call_ret.oo:225-253`) maps env/fs/fetch/exec/walk/dir
names. It does **not** map `fs_mkdir_p`, `fs_copy_tree`, `fs_copy_file`,
`fs_hardlink`, `fs_symlink`. Those are non-cap misses if they appear as
annotated let-init / `return f()` / mut assign.

`check_drive.oo:109,111` still calls both lazy walkers on every
`run_check_core_binds` (np>12 = once per module body).

## 2 — first non-cap still builds the full table (12 GiB residual)
Honest leftover for later RSS work:

1. The builder is unchanged. First miss in let/ret is the **same** full
   concat table that used to run at entry.
2. Lazy is per-walker, per-module. Each of the two functions may build
   once. They do not share a table.
3. Most other check walkers are **still eager** — they never got the
   `""` sentinel. Product `check oodac/main.oo` still pays them even if
   every let/return call is a cap name.

Eager / in-loop (still paid; not lazy):

| Caller | Site | When |
|--------|------|------|
| `ann_prep` → `typecheck_ann_and_return_lits` | `tc_ann_prep.oo:16` | every module (`check_drive.oo:83` first typecheck) |
| `call_arg_prep` → `typecheck_call_arg_lits` | `tc_call_arg_prep.oo:15` | every module (`check_drive.oo:107`) |
| `typecheck_call_eq_lits` | `tc_call_eq.oo:14` | every module (`:113`) |
| `typecheck_call_order_lits` | `tc_call_order.oo:14` | every module (`:115`) |
| `typecheck_call_binop_lits` | `tc_call_binop.oo:14` | every module (`:117`) |
| `typecheck_call_logic_lits` | `tc_call_logic.oo:14` | every module (`:118`) |
| `typecheck_field_method` | `tc_struct_method.oo:15` | every module (`:120`) |
| unary | `tc_ops_unary.oo:15` and `:92` | twice per module |
| `if g()` / `while g()` | `tc_control_cond.oo:200` | **inside** the cond walk |
| untyped `let x = g()` | `tc_tables.oo:208` | **inside** the let walk |

np>12 tokenizes per module, so each table is module-sized, not a 150-file
concat. Still: one module pays the builder many times. That is check
**churn**. It is not proved to be the 12 GiB (B8 leftover is still the
named pin). Killing the two entry-time builds in let/ret does **not**
remove the table, and does **not** make check cheap without a new measure.

This turn did **not** run `oodac check oodac/main.oo`. Cite only
(prior INT, not re-timed): 26.30s / 12854108 kB = **12.26 GiB**, rc 0.
Still **>** F26 WARN 8 GiB. Still **<** FAIL 32 GiB.

## 3 — fs_mkdir_p is not fs_walk
`/home/jeryd/Projects/openOODA/ooda/std/os/fs.oo:103-135`

Linear prefix walk of **one** path String. Not a directory recurse.

- `n = chars_len(path)`; `while i < n`; every arm `i = i + 1`.
- Accumulates `acc` char-by-char; on `/` (and at end) calls `fs_mkdir(fw, acc)`.
- `fs_mkdir` is a C one-shot (`oo_fs_mkdir`). No self-call.
- Existing-dir Err is ignored (`let _ = fs_mkdir(...)`); comment `:102`.
- No depth. No tree. Bound is **path length**, not tree depth.
- `acc = acc + c` is O(n²) copies of a path-sized String. Ugly; not a
  12 GiB source. C `to_cpath` still clamps each mkdir to `PATH_MAX`
  (`chs_rt_fs.c:133-141`).
- No `// MAX_CYCLES` on `fs.oo`. Loop still terminates: `i` always moves.

**Not the same residual as `fs_walk`.**

`sh_mkdir_p` (`std/sh.oo:47-51`) is this function after `sh_arg_ok`.

## 4 — fs_copy_tree is the same residual as fs_walk
`fs_walk` (`fs.oo:50-76`): if `fs_is_dir`, `fs_read_dir`, then
`fs_walk(fs, sub_path)` per entry. Push every path. No depth arg.
No visited set. Named leftover since sprint12 / typestate audit.

`fs_copy_tree` (`fs.oo:150-169`): **same walk**.

```
if fs_is_dir(src):
  fs_mkdir_p(dst)
  for name in fs_read_dir(src):
    fs_copy_tree(src + "/" + name, dst + "/" + name)
else:
  fs_copy_file(src, dst)   # full-file String read + write
```

Same holes as walk

- No depth cap. Recursion depth = tree depth. Deep tree can blow the stack.
- No visited set. No cycle detect.
- `oo_fs_is_dir` uses `stat` (`chs_rt_fs.c:117-121`), which **follows**
  symlinks. `oo_fs_read_dir` skips `.` and `..` only (`:107`). A dirent
  symlink to an ancestor is infinite recursion, not just a deep stack.
- No `// MAX_CYCLES` on this file. Fuel is opt-in per TU
  (`bootstrap/MAX_CYCLES.oot`). Default walk/copy is uncapped.
- `fs_read_dir` is List[String] of names; `fs_walk` then copies the
  whole child list into `out` (walk-only extra heap). `fs_copy_tree`
  instead holds each file body (`fs_copy_file:142-147`).

Extra vs walk: every directory pays `fs_mkdir_p` (linear, fine); every
file pays a full-content String. Heap follows the **largest file**, plus
stack frames. Still unbounded in tree shape.

`sh_copy` (`std/sh.oo:54-59`) is `fs_copy_tree` after `sh_arg_ok`.

`cap_builtin_ret` maps `fs_walk` / `fs_read_dir` → `List` and `fs_mkdir`
→ `Result`. It does **not** map `fs_copy_tree` / `fs_mkdir_p`. Check of
callers that annotate those returns is a first-miss table build.

## Residual
| Item | Sev | Note |
|------|-----|------|
| **first non-cap miss = full ret_table** | MED | Lazy is defer, not delete. Same concat builder. Honest 12 GiB leftover. |
| N× eager `build_fn_ret_table` per module | MED | siblings + in-loop cond/let. Unproven share of the 12 GiB. |
| **12 GiB still > 8 GiB WARN** | MED | Board pin. Not re-measured. **Not closed.** |
| `fs_copy_tree` no depth / no cycle cap | MED | Same class as `fs_walk`. `stat` follows symlinks. |
| `fs_walk` no depth cap | MED (old) | `:50-76`. Unchanged. |
| `fs_mkdir_p` path concat O(n²) | LOW | Linear in path chars. Not a tree walk. |
| `cap_builtin_ret` misses mkdir_p/copy_tree | LOW | Annotated use is a table-build trigger. |
| unannotated let still drops `gty` | LOW | Prior B6 hole. Does not save a sibling eager build. |

**MEM return:** lazy ret_table does **not** remove the table; first non-cap
call still builds the full concat table (honest residual for 12 GiB later).
`fs_mkdir_p` is a bounded prefix walk. `fs_copy_tree` is an unbounded
recurse, same as `fs_walk`.

end of sprint_mem_b6_fs.oot
